# Advanced QFT and supersymmetry: non-Abelian gauge theory research scaffold

Research date: 2026-10-23. This note is a prose and prerequisite scaffold for a proposed physics category. It is not an authored physics item, proof acceptance, independent review, source audit, or production-readiness record. No physics build stage was launched. The category remains subject to the ordinary physics plan, supplier snapshot, authoring, review, and gate workflow.

The scope here is the geometric and mathematical development of non-Abelian Yang–Mills theory: principal bundles and connection forms; local gauge potentials and curvature; the classical Yang–Mills action; gauge fixing, Faddeev–Popov, BRST and BV; perturbative gauge-preserving renormalization; topological sectors and instantons; lattice observables and confinement; and the four-dimensional existence/mass-gap frontier. General EFT and anomaly-matching interfaces belong to the adjacent scaffold, while supersymmetry transformations, supermultiplets, BPS sectors, localization, and dualities belong to the supersymmetry scaffold. This note proposes a few shared gauge suppliers for those tracks but does not take ownership of their distinct results.

## Scope, assumptions, and authoring boundary

The authors should begin with an explicitly selected compact Lie group \(G\), including its global form, a finite-dimensional unitary representation \(R\) for every matter multiplet, a principal right \(G\)-bundle \(P\to M\), and the spacetime geometry. The global group is part of the physical model. A Lie algebra alone does not identify the global gauge group or its allowed representations: \(SU(N)\), \(PSU(N)\), and other central quotients can have the same Lie algebra and different line operators and charge lattices. Do not use a group label such as “\(SU(N)\) gauge theory” as shorthand for both the local Lie algebra and the chosen bundle topology.

Use two separate settings. The Lorentzian setting is an oriented and time-oriented four-dimensional Lorentz manifold or a stated flat region with metric signature \((-+++)\); it defines the local classical action and the perturbative physical model. The Euclidean instanton setting is an oriented Riemannian four-manifold, often \(S^4\) or \(\mathbb R^4\) with a stated finite-action condition at infinity. Hodge star, self-duality, elliptic regularity, and the Euclidean action bound belong to that second setting. A Euclidean saddle is not a Lorentzian particle solution, and a formal Wick rotation does not establish a quantum theory.

For a chosen invariant positive-definite inner product on \(\mathfrak g\), specify the normalization of the action, trace, coupling, topological charge, and theta term. In four dimensions and natural units, the gauge field has engineering dimension one, its curvature dimension two, the coupling dimension zero, and the action density dimension four. These assignments are model conventions. The author must also give the quantity types, physical roles, units, suppressed constants, and scope required by PHYSICS-CONTENT-MODEL.md. No measured value, empirical claim, or continuum limit follows from a formal classical or regulated model.

Every definition and result must use exact hypotheses and local arguments. Sources document the mathematical and physical context; a citation is not a proof supplier in active physics content. A locally authored theorem must prove its stated implication from the declared mathematical premises and physical postulates. A formal perturbation series is coefficientwise algebra and carries no convergence claim. A regulator result is not a continuum result. An established theorem whose proof is not written locally is an authoring obligation, not an open problem. Genuinely unresolved claims use an op- item, receive a dated formulation/status/source review, and never become logical suppliers.

## Existing mathematical suppliers, by current files

The exact imported mathematical item inventory is physics/research/math-imports.json. The rows below were checked against that file and the actual current item/page carriers. They identify reusable scope; future work must still bind the exact supplier bytes and hypotheses in its ordinary plan.

| Current item and page | Current status and available scope | Boundary to preserve |
|---|---|---|
| **def-principal-g-bundle-and-associated-fiber-bundle**; **def-smooth-principal-g-bundle** on **library/lie-theory/gauge-groups-and-representation-theory-interfaces.md** | Both root items are published and listed in the physics math-import snapshot. The first gives the topological right-principal-bundle and associated-bundle quotient; the second adds a finite-dimensional real Lie group, smooth right action, smooth projection, and equivariant local principal charts. | Smoothness and local triviality do not give a global trivialization or a connection. A principal-bundle classification statement requires its own numerability and base-space hypotheses. |
| **def-principal-connection-and-vertical-curvature**; **def-gauge-group-of-a-smooth-principal-bundle**; **prop-gauge-transformations-act-on-principal-connections**; **prop-gauge-transformations-induce-associated-bundle-automorphisms** on the same page | Published and imported. The connection is a right-invariant horizontal complement \(TP=VP\oplus H\); its curvature is the vertical-valued tensor \(K_H(X,Y)=-v([hX,hY])\). The gauge group consists of smooth equivariant diffeomorphisms over \(\mathrm{id}_M\). Pushforward acts on horizontal connections and transports vertical curvature; a finite-dimensional representation gives the induced associated-bundle automorphism and section action. | The connection definition explicitly does not identify \(VP\) with \(\mathfrak g\), state a \(\mathfrak g\)-valued connection one-form, or give Cartan’s formula \(F=dA+\tfrac12[A\wedge A]\). The gauge-group definition assigns no topology or infinite-dimensional Lie-group structure. The connection and connection-action chain inherits the stated \(\mathrm{AC}_\omega\) assumptions; the gauge-group definition itself is choice-free. Carry each axiom only through consumers that actually use that supplier. |
| **def-lie-group**, **def-lie-algebra-over-a-field**, **def-adjoint-representation-of-a-lie-algebra**, and **prop-adjoint-is-a-smooth-lie-group-representation** on **library/lie-theory/lie-groups-invariant-fields-and-the-exponential-map.md** and **library/lie-theory/lie-algebra-representations-enveloping-algebras-and-pbw.md** | The root carriers are published and the relevant IDs are present in the physics import snapshot. They supply the finite-dimensional Lie group/Lie algebra conventions, conjugation and adjoint action, and representation prerequisites. | These finite-dimensional objects do not by themselves give an infinite-dimensional space of gauge transformations, its topology, a gauge-slice theorem, or a path-integral measure. The Lie-algebra representation does not select a global form of \(G\). |
| **def-associated-bundle-to-a-principal-bundle-and-representation**; **thm-associated-vector-bundle-is-well-defined**; **def-curvature-of-a-vector-bundle-connection**; **thm-curvature-two-form-structure-equation**; **thm-local-connection-forms-glue-exactly-when-they-obey-the-transformation-law** | The first three and the cited local connection suppliers are mathematical items in the imported/current corpus. The associated-bundle items give a bundle and connection target for matter representations; the vector-bundle connection items prove the local matrix gluing law for an already defined vector-bundle connection. | The local matrix-gluing theorem is not a principal \(\mathfrak g\)-valued gauge-potential theorem. Do not silently identify a vector-bundle frame change with a gauge transformation on a nontrivial principal bundle. |
| **def-invariant-polynomial-on-a-matrix-lie-algebra**, **def-evaluation-of-an-invariant-polynomial-on-curvature**, **lem-an-invariant-polynomial-of-curvature-is-closed**, **def-the-chern-weil-homomorphism**, **thm-chern-weil-homomorphism-is-independent-of-connection-and-natural**, and **thm-chern-weil-forms-represent-the-at-characteristic-classes-over-the-reals** on **library/differential-geometry/chern-weil-theory-and-characteristic-forms.md** | These mathematical root items are published and occur in the physics snapshot. They establish global closed invariant curvature forms, connection independence and naturality of the de Rham classes, and identification with the real-coefficient characteristic class. | They do not make a real characteristic form integral for an arbitrary group or trace normalization. They do not detect integral torsion. A normalized instanton number and its integer quantization need a stated group, bundle, representation/trace convention, and an integral characteristic-class argument. Full AC is used only where the cited connection-existence and comparison results say it is used; supplied-connection statements retain their narrower assumptions. |
