           "kind": "definition",
           "domain": "physics",
           "title": "The associated first-order system, motions, maximal motions and the Newtonian flow of a force model",
-          "statement": "Let a force model of [[post-cm02-force-superposition]] be given: time window\n$I_{0}\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ nondegenerate and open, configuration domain\n$Q\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{3N}$ open ([[def-cm01-configuration]] clause (a)),\nfixed masses $m_{1},\\dots,m_{N}>0$ ([[post-cm01-worldlines]] clause (v)), and a\nforce map $F$ with the standing continuity and state-local-Lipschitz\nhypotheses of clause (iii) of that postulate.\n\n**(a) State domain and inverse-mass operator.** The **state domain** of the\nmodel is\n\n$$D:=I_{0}\\times Q\\times(\\mathbb R^{3})^{N}\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^{3N}\\times\\mathbb R^{3N},$$\n\nan open subset of $\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^{6N}$; a point $(t,x,v)\\in D$ is a\ntime together with the **state** $z=(x,v)$ of positions $x\\in Q$ and coordinate\nvelocities $v\\in(\\mathbb R^{3})^{N}$ of the labels ([[def-cm01-velocity-acceleration]],\n[[def-cm01-units]]). The **inverse-mass operator** is the linear map\n\n$$\\mathsf M^{-1}:\\mathbb R^{3N}\\to\\mathbb R^{3N},\\qquad \\mathsf M^{-1}(u_{1},\\dots,u_{N}):=\\Bigl(\\frac{u_{1}}{m_{1}},\\dots,\\frac{u_{N}}{m_{N}}\\Bigr),$$\n\nthe inverse of the mass operator $\\mathsf M$ of clause (d) of\n[[def-cm01-configuration]].\n\n**(b) Associated first-order system.** The **associated first-order field** of\nthe model is\n\n$$\\mathcal F:D\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R^{3N}\\times\\mathbb R^{3N},\\qquad \\mathcal F(t,x,v):=\\Bigl(v,\\ \\mathsf M^{-1}F(t,x,v)\\Bigr),$$\n\nand the **associated first-order system** is $z'=\\mathcal F(t,z)$ on the open\ndomain $D$, in the sense of\n[[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]]; here\n$z=(x,v)\\in\\mathbb R^{6N}$. The map $\\mathcal F$ satisfies the\nPicard–Lindelöf hypotheses of\n[[thm-picard-lindelof-local-existence-and-uniqueness]]: it is continuous on\n$D$, and it is locally Lipschitz in the state variable $z$ locally uniformly\nin time, because its first component $z\\mapsto v$ is linear and its second is\nthe composition of the force map $F$ — which is locally Lipschitz in $(x,v)$\nlocally uniformly in time by [[post-cm02-force-superposition]] clause (iii) —\nwith the linear map $\\mathsf M^{-1}$.\n\n**(c) Motions.** Let $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval with nonempty\ninterior, endpoints read with one-sided derivatives as in\n[[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]]. A **motion** of\nthe model on $J$ is a curve $x:J\\to Q$ of class $C^{2}$ such that\n$t\\in I_{0}$ for every $t\\in J$ and\n\n$$m_{i}\\,\\ddot x_{i}(t)=F_{i}\\bigl(t,x(t),\\dot x(t)\\bigr)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots,N,\\ t\\in J).$$\n\nEquivalently: the state curve $z=(x,\\dot x):J\\to D$ is a solution of the\nassociated first-order system on $J$. Indeed, if $x$ is a motion then\n$z=(x,x')$ is $C^{1}$ with\n$z'=(x',x'')=(x',\\mathsf M^{-1}F(t,x,x'))=\\mathcal F(t,z)$ and $z(t)\\in D$;\nconversely, if $z=(x,v)$ is a solution with $z(t)\\in D$ for all $t$, then\n$x'=v$ and $v'=\\mathsf M^{-1}F(t,x,v)$, so $v$ is $C^{1}$, $x$ is $C^{2}$, and\nmultiplying the second equation by the masses returns the displayed\nsecond-order equation. Motions of the labels of the model are exactly these\ncurves by [[post-cm02-force-superposition]] clause (iv).\n\n**(d) Initial data and maximal motion.** An **initial datum** of the model is\na triple $(t_{0},x_{0},v_{0})$ with $t_{0}\\in I_{0}$, $x_{0}\\in Q$ and\n$v_{0}\\in(\\mathbb R^{3})^{N}$; write $z_{0}:=(x_{0},v_{0})$. A **maximal\nmotion** with this datum is a motion $x:J_{\\max}\\to Q$ with $x(t_{0})=x_{0}$\nand $\\dot x(t_{0})=v_{0}$ that has no extension to a motion of the model on a\nstrictly larger interval. By the Picard–Lindelöf hypotheses verified in (b)\nand the maximal-solution theorem\n[[thm-existence-and-uniqueness-of-a-maximal-ode-solution]] (with\n[[def-maximal-ode-solution]]): every initial datum has exactly one maximal\nmotion, its **maximal interval** $J_{\\max}$ is an open interval containing\n$t_{0}$ and contained in $I_{0}$, and every motion with the same datum is the\nrestriction of the maximal one. The restriction statement transfers from the\nassociated first-order system to motions through the equivalence in (c).\n\n**(e) Newtonian flow.** The **Newtonian flow** of the model is the map\n\n$$\\Phi:\\Omega\\longrightarrow Q\\times\\mathbb R^{3N},\\qquad \\Omega:=\\bigl\\{(t,t_{0},z_{0}):t_{0}\\in I_{0},\\ z_{0}\\in Q\\times\\mathbb R^{3N},\\ t\\in J_{\\max}(t_{0},z_{0})\\bigr\\},$$\n\ndefined by $\\Phi(t;t_{0},z_{0}):=z(t)$, where $t\\mapsto z(t)=(x(t),\\dot x(t))$\nis the state curve of the maximal motion with datum $(t_{0},z_{0})$. By (d) the\nassignment is well defined: the maximal motion is unique, so $\\Phi$ is a\nfunction on $\\Omega$ and not merely a relation. For fixed $t_{0}$ and $z_{0}$\nthe curve $t\\mapsto\\Phi(t;t_{0},z_{0})$ is defined exactly on the maximal\ninterval $J_{\\max}(t_{0},z_{0})$.",
+          "statement": "Let a force model of [[post-cm02-force-superposition]] be given: time window\n$I_{0}\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ nondegenerate and open, configuration domain\n$Q\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{3N}$ open ([[def-cm01-configuration]] clause (a)),\nfixed masses $m_{1},\\dots,m_{N}>0$ ([[post-cm01-worldlines]] clause (v)), and a\nforce map $F$ with the standing continuity and state-local-Lipschitz\nhypotheses of clause (iii) of that postulate.\n\n**(a) State domain and inverse-mass operator.** The **state domain** of the\nmodel is\n\n$$D:=I_{0}\\times Q\\times(\\mathbb R^{3})^{N}\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^{3N}\\times\\mathbb R^{3N},$$\n\nan open subset of $\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^{6N}$; a point $(t,x,v)\\in D$ is a\ntime together with the **state** $z=(x,v)$ of positions $x\\in Q$ and coordinate\nvelocities $v\\in(\\mathbb R^{3})^{N}$ of the labels ([[def-cm01-velocity-acceleration]],\n[[def-cm01-units]]). The **inverse-mass operator** is the linear map\n\n$$\\mathsf M^{-1}:\\mathbb R^{3N}\\to\\mathbb R^{3N},\\qquad \\mathsf M^{-1}(u_{1},\\dots,u_{N}):=\\Bigl(\\frac{u_{1}}{m_{1}},\\dots,\\frac{u_{N}}{m_{N}}\\Bigr),$$\n\nthe inverse of the mass operator $\\mathsf M$ of clause (d) of\n[[def-cm01-configuration]].\n\n**(b) Associated first-order system.** The **associated first-order field** of\nthe model is\n\n$$\\mathcal F:D\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R^{3N}\\times\\mathbb R^{3N},\\qquad \\mathcal F(t,x,v):=\\Bigl(v,\\ \\mathsf M^{-1}F(t,x,v)\\Bigr),$$\n\nand the **associated first-order system** is $z'=\\mathcal F(t,z)$ on the open\ndomain $D$, in the sense of\n[[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]]; here\n$z=(x,v)\\in\\mathbb R^{6N}$. The map $\\mathcal F$ satisfies the\nPicard\u2013Lindel\u00f6f hypotheses of\n[[thm-picard-lindelof-local-existence-and-uniqueness]]: it is continuous on\n$D$, and it is locally Lipschitz in the state variable $z$ locally uniformly\nin time, because its first component $z\\mapsto v$ is linear and its second is\nthe composition of the force map $F$ \u2014 which is locally Lipschitz in $(x,v)$\nlocally uniformly in time by [[post-cm02-force-superposition]] clause (iii) \u2014\nwith the linear map $\\mathsf M^{-1}$.\n\n**(c) Motions.** Let $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval with nonempty\ninterior, endpoints read with one-sided derivatives as in\n[[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]]. A **motion** of\nthe model on $J$ is a curve $x:J\\to Q$ of class $C^{2}$ such that\n$t\\in I_{0}$ for every $t\\in J$ and\n\n$$m_{i}\\,\\ddot x_{i}(t)=F_{i}\\bigl(t,x(t),\\dot x(t)\\bigr)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots,N,\\ t\\in J).$$\n\nEquivalently: the state curve $z=(x,\\dot x):J\\to D$ is a solution of the\nassociated first-order system on $J$. Indeed, if $x$ is a motion then\n$z=(x,x')$ is $C^{1}$ with\n$z'=(x',x'')=(x',\\mathsf M^{-1}F(t,x,x'))=\\mathcal F(t,z)$ and $z(t)\\in D$;\nconversely, if $z=(x,v)$ is a solution with $z(t)\\in D$ for all $t$, then\n$x'=v$ and $v'=\\mathsf M^{-1}F(t,x,v)$, so $v$ is $C^{1}$, $x$ is $C^{2}$, and\nmultiplying the second equation by the masses returns the displayed\nsecond-order equation. Motions of the labels of the model are exactly these\ncurves by [[post-cm02-force-superposition]] clause (iv).\n\n**(d) Initial data and maximal motion.** An **initial datum** of the model is\na triple $(t_{0},x_{0},v_{0})$ with $t_{0}\\in I_{0}$, $x_{0}\\in Q$ and\n$v_{0}\\in(\\mathbb R^{3})^{N}$; write $z_{0}:=(x_{0},v_{0})$. A **maximal\nmotion** with this datum is a motion $x:J_{\\max}\\to Q$ with $x(t_{0})=x_{0}$\nand $\\dot x(t_{0})=v_{0}$ that has no extension to a motion of the model on a\nstrictly larger interval. By the Picard\u2013Lindel\u00f6f hypotheses verified in (b)\nand the maximal-solution theorem\n[[thm-existence-and-uniqueness-of-a-maximal-ode-solution]] (with\n[[def-maximal-ode-solution]]): every initial datum has exactly one maximal\nmotion, its **maximal interval** $J_{\\max}$ is an open interval containing\n$t_{0}$ and contained in $I_{0}$, and every motion with the same datum is the\nrestriction of the maximal one. The restriction statement transfers from the\nassociated first-order system to motions through the equivalence in (c).\n\n**(e) Newtonian flow.** The **Newtonian flow** of the model is the map\n\n$$\\Phi:\\Omega\\longrightarrow Q\\times\\mathbb R^{3N},\\qquad \\Omega:=\\bigl\\{(t,t_{0},z_{0}):t_{0}\\in I_{0},\\ z_{0}\\in Q\\times\\mathbb R^{3N},\\ t\\in J_{\\max}(t_{0},z_{0})\\bigr\\},$$\n\ndefined by $\\Phi(t;t_{0},z_{0}):=z(t)$, where $t\\mapsto z(t)=(x(t),\\dot x(t))$\nis the state curve of the maximal motion with datum $(t_{0},z_{0})$. By (d) the\nassignment is well defined: the maximal motion is unique, so $\\Phi$ is a\nfunction on $\\Omega$ and not merely a relation. For fixed $t_{0}$ and $z_{0}$\nthe curve $t\\mapsto\\Phi(t;t_{0},z_{0})$ is defined exactly on the maximal\ninterval $J_{\\max}(t_{0},z_{0})$.",
           "strategy": "A definition: it fixes the state domain, the inverse-mass operator, the associated first-order field and system, motions and their equivalence with the second-order equation, initial data, the maximal motion and its maximal interval (existence by the cited maximal-solution theorem), and the Newtonian flow map; regularity of the flow is left to separate results.",
           "deps": [
             "post-cm02-force-superposition",
@@ -22243,7 +22243,7 @@
           "id": "pthm-cm02-ivp",
           "kind": "physics-theorem",
           "domain": "physics",
-          "title": "Local existence and uniqueness for the Newtonian initial-value problem, with the quantitative Picard-Lindelöf cylinder",
+          "title": "Local existence and uniqueness for the Newtonian initial-value problem, with the quantitative Picard-Lindel\u00f6f cylinder",
           "statement": "Adopt the force model of [[post-cm02-force-superposition]]: time window\n$I_{0}$, open configuration domain $Q\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{3N}$, masses\n$m_{1},\\dots,m_{N}>0$, and a force map $F$ continuous and locally Lipschitz in\nthe state variable, locally uniformly in time, on\n$D=I_{0}\\times Q\\times(\\mathbb R^{3})^{N}$. Let\n$\\mathcal F(t,x,v)=\\bigl(v,\\mathsf M^{-1}F(t,x,v)\\bigr)$ be the associated\nfirst-order field and let motions and maximal motions be as in\n[[def-cm02-newtonian-flow]]. Fix an initial datum\n$(t_{0},x_{0},v_{0})\\in I_{0}\\times Q\\times(\\mathbb R^{3})^{N}$ and write\n$z_{0}=(x_{0},v_{0})$.\n\n1. **Local existence.** There is $h>0$ and there is a motion $x$ of the model\n   on $[t_{0}-h,t_{0}+h]$ with $x(t_{0})=x_{0}$ and $\\dot x(t_{0})=v_{0}$;\n   moreover such a motion is exactly one of class $C^{2}$ satisfying\n   $m_{i}\\ddot x_{i}(t)=F_{i}(t,x(t),\\dot x(t))$ for all $i$ and all\n   $t\\in[t_{0}-h,t_{0}+h]$.\n2. **Uniqueness.** If $x:J\\to Q$ and $\\tilde x:\\tilde J\\to Q$ are motions of\n   the model with the same initial datum,\n   $x(t_{0})=\\tilde x(t_{0})=x_{0}$ and $\\dot x(t_{0})=\\dot{\\tilde x}(t_{0})=v_{0}$,\n   then $x=\\tilde x$ on $J\\cap\\tilde J$.\n3. **Quantitative cylinder.** Let $h,r>0$ satisfy\n   $C:=[t_{0}-h,t_{0}+h]\\times\\overline B(z_{0},r)\\subseteq D$, put\n   $M:=\\sup\\{\\lVert\\mathcal F(t,z)\\rVert_{2}:(t,z)\\in C\\}$ and let $\\Lambda$\n   be a state-Lipschitz constant of $\\mathcal F$ on $C$. If $hM\\le r$ and\n   $\\Lambda h<1$, then there is exactly one motion on $[t_{0}-h,t_{0}+h]$\n   through the datum whose state graph $(t,(x(t),\\dot x(t)))$ lies in $C$, and\n   every motion through the datum agrees with it on the intersection of its\n   interval with $[t_{0}-h,t_{0}+h]$.\n4. **Maximal motion.** There is exactly one maximal motion with the datum:\n   its maximal interval $J_{\\max}$ is an open interval containing $t_{0}$ and\n   contained in $I_{0}$, and every motion with the datum is its restriction.\n\nThe statement is conditional on the adopted force model: clause 1 locates a\nsolution only near $t_{0}$ and bounds no interval length in terms of the data,\nclause 4 asserts no completeness of $J_{\\max}$ in $I_{0}$, and no empirical\ncontent is claimed.",
           "strategy": "A conditional deduction: verify that the associated first-order field is continuous and locally Lipschitz on the open state domain, apply Picard-Lindelof in its quantitative cylinder form, translate the solution back to the second-order motion, prove uniqueness on common intervals, and obtain the unique maximal motion by the maximal-solution theorem.",
           "deps": [
@@ -22300,7 +22300,7 @@
           "kind": "physics-theorem",
           "domain": "physics",
           "title": "Maximal Newtonian motions and the compact-continuation criterion: finite-time breakdown requires escape from every compact subset of the time-state domain",
-          "statement": "Adopt the force model of [[post-cm02-force-superposition]] with time window\n$I_{0}$, open configuration domain $Q\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{3N}$, masses\n$m_{1},\\dots,m_{N}>0$ and force map $F$ continuous and locally Lipschitz in the\nstate variable, locally uniformly in time; let $D=I_{0}\\times Q\\times(\\mathbb\nR^{3})^{N}$, $\\mathcal F(t,x,v)=\\bigl(v,\\mathsf M^{-1}F(t,x,v)\\bigr)$,\n$U:=Q\\times\\mathbb R^{3N}$, and let motions, maximal motions and the state\ncurve $z=(x,\\dot x)$ be as in [[def-cm02-newtonian-flow]]. Fix an initial datum\n$(t_{0},x_{0},v_{0})\\in I_{0}\\times Q\\times(\\mathbb R^{3})^{N}$, write\n$z_{0}=(x_{0},v_{0})$, and let $x_{\\max}:J_{\\max}\\to Q$ be the maximal motion\nwith this datum, with state curve $z=(x_{\\max},\\dot x_{\\max}):J_{\\max}\\to U$\nand $J_{\\max}=(a,b)$ its maximal interval, $-\\infty\\le a<b\\le+\\infty$.\n\n1. **Existence and uniqueness of the maximal motion.** $J_{\\max}$ is an open\n   interval containing $t_{0}$ and contained in $I_{0}$, and every motion with\n   the datum is a restriction of $x_{\\max}$.\n2. **Breakdown criterion.** If $b<+\\infty$, then for every compact\n   $K\\subseteq D$ and every $\\eta>0$ with $(b-\\eta,b)\\cap J_{\\max}\\neq\\varnothing$\n   there is $t\\in(b-\\eta,b)\\cap J_{\\max}$ with $(t,z(t))\\notin K$. Equivalently:\n   if there are a compact $K\\subseteq D$ and $\\delta>0$ with\n   $(t,z(t))\\in K$ for all $t\\in(b-\\delta,b)\\cap J_{\\max}$, then\n   $x_{\\max}$ extends to a motion of the model on a strictly larger interval\n   than $J_{\\max}$ — so $J_{\\max}$ cannot have right endpoint $b$.\n3. **Autonomous case.** Suppose $F$ does not depend on its time argument:\n   $F(t,x,v)=F_{0}(x,v)$ for a map $F_{0}:U\\to(\\mathbb R^{3})^{N}$ satisfying\n   the standing regularity of [[post-cm02-force-superposition]] clause (iii).\n   Then the associated field is autonomous,\n   $\\mathcal F(t,x,v)=(v,\\mathsf M^{-1}F_{0}(x,v))$, the equations do not use\n   the time coordinate, and **the model is read on the extended time window\n   $\\mathbb R$** (the standing hypotheses are unchanged by the extension).\n   For this clause, take the same autonomous force law as a model on the\n   extended time window $I_{0}=\\mathbb R$, and let $J_{\\max}=(a,b)$ be the\n   maximal interval for that extended-time model. If $b<+\\infty$ and there\n   are a compact $K_{0}\\subseteq U$ and $\\delta>0$ with $z(t)\\in K_{0}$ for\n   all $t\\in(b-\\delta,b)\\cap J_{\\max}$, then the state curve extends past\n   $b$ as a solution of the same model, contradicting maximality; thus no\n   finite maximal endpoint has such a terminal compact trap. In particular,\n   if one compact $K_{0}\\subseteq U$ contains $z(t)$ for every\n   $t\\in J_{\\max}\\cap[t_{0},+\\infty)$, then $b=+\\infty$ and the motion is\n   defined for all forward times $t\\ge t_{0}$.\n4. **Reading.** Suppose the time window is all of $\\mathbb R$ (the\n   time-dependent case included) and $b<+\\infty$. Then for every compact\n   $K_{0}\\subseteq U$ and every $\\delta>0$ there is $t\\in(b-\\delta,b)\\cap J_{\\max}$ with\n   $z(t)\\notin K_{0}$: near a finite breakdown the state leaves every compact\n   subset of the open state domain $U=Q\\times\\mathbb R^{3N}$. Consequently\n   either the state is unbounded on every terminal interval, meaning that for\n   every $M,\\delta>0$ some $t\\in(b-\\delta,b)\\cap J_{\\max}$ has\n   $\\lVert z(t)\\rVert_{2}>M$, or $Q\\ne\\mathbb R^{3N}$ and the positions\n   approach the excluded configurations in the following sense: for every\n   $\\varepsilon,\\delta>0$ there are $t\\in(b-\\delta,b)\\cap J_{\\max}$ and\n   $y\\in\\mathbb R^{3N}\\setminus Q$ with\n   $\\lVert x_{\\max}(t)-y\\rVert_{2}<\\varepsilon$. If $Q=\\mathbb R^{3N}$, the\n   unbounded-state alternative necessarily holds.",
+          "statement": "Adopt the force model of [[post-cm02-force-superposition]] with time window\n$I_{0}$, open configuration domain $Q\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{3N}$, masses\n$m_{1},\\dots,m_{N}>0$ and force map $F$ continuous and locally Lipschitz in the\nstate variable, locally uniformly in time; let $D=I_{0}\\times Q\\times(\\mathbb\nR^{3})^{N}$, $\\mathcal F(t,x,v)=\\bigl(v,\\mathsf M^{-1}F(t,x,v)\\bigr)$,\n$U:=Q\\times\\mathbb R^{3N}$, and let motions, maximal motions and the state\ncurve $z=(x,\\dot x)$ be as in [[def-cm02-newtonian-flow]]. Fix an initial datum\n$(t_{0},x_{0},v_{0})\\in I_{0}\\times Q\\times(\\mathbb R^{3})^{N}$, write\n$z_{0}=(x_{0},v_{0})$, and let $x_{\\max}:J_{\\max}\\to Q$ be the maximal motion\nwith this datum, with state curve $z=(x_{\\max},\\dot x_{\\max}):J_{\\max}\\to U$\nand $J_{\\max}=(a,b)$ its maximal interval, $-\\infty\\le a<b\\le+\\infty$.\n\n1. **Existence and uniqueness of the maximal motion.** $J_{\\max}$ is an open\n   interval containing $t_{0}$ and contained in $I_{0}$, and every motion with\n   the datum is a restriction of $x_{\\max}$.\n2. **Breakdown criterion.** If $b<+\\infty$, then for every compact\n   $K\\subseteq D$ and every $\\eta>0$ with $(b-\\eta,b)\\cap J_{\\max}\\neq\\varnothing$\n   there is $t\\in(b-\\eta,b)\\cap J_{\\max}$ with $(t,z(t))\\notin K$. Equivalently:\n   if there are a compact $K\\subseteq D$ and $\\delta>0$ with\n   $(t,z(t))\\in K$ for all $t\\in(b-\\delta,b)\\cap J_{\\max}$, then\n   $x_{\\max}$ extends to a motion of the model on a strictly larger interval\n   than $J_{\\max}$ \u2014 so $J_{\\max}$ cannot have right endpoint $b$.\n3. **Autonomous case.** Suppose $F$ does not depend on its time argument:\n   $F(t,x,v)=F_{0}(x,v)$ for a map $F_{0}:U\\to(\\mathbb R^{3})^{N}$ satisfying\n   the standing regularity of [[post-cm02-force-superposition]] clause (iii).\n   Then the associated field is autonomous,\n   $\\mathcal F(t,x,v)=(v,\\mathsf M^{-1}F_{0}(x,v))$, the equations do not use\n   the time coordinate, and **the model is read on the extended time window\n   $\\mathbb R$** (the standing hypotheses are unchanged by the extension).\n   For this clause, take the same autonomous force law as a model on the\n   extended time window $I_{0}=\\mathbb R$, and let $J_{\\max}=(a,b)$ be the\n   maximal interval for that extended-time model. If $b<+\\infty$ and there\n   are a compact $K_{0}\\subseteq U$ and $\\delta>0$ with $z(t)\\in K_{0}$ for\n   all $t\\in(b-\\delta,b)\\cap J_{\\max}$, then the state curve extends past\n   $b$ as a solution of the same model, contradicting maximality; thus no\n   finite maximal endpoint has such a terminal compact trap. In particular,\n   if one compact $K_{0}\\subseteq U$ contains $z(t)$ for every\n   $t\\in J_{\\max}\\cap[t_{0},+\\infty)$, then $b=+\\infty$ and the motion is\n   defined for all forward times $t\\ge t_{0}$.\n4. **Reading.** Suppose the time window is all of $\\mathbb R$ (the\n   time-dependent case included) and $b<+\\infty$. Then for every compact\n   $K_{0}\\subseteq U$ and every $\\delta>0$ there is $t\\in(b-\\delta,b)\\cap J_{\\max}$ with\n   $z(t)\\notin K_{0}$: near a finite breakdown the state leaves every compact\n   subset of the open state domain $U=Q\\times\\mathbb R^{3N}$. Consequently\n   either the state is unbounded on every terminal interval, meaning that for\n   every $M,\\delta>0$ some $t\\in(b-\\delta,b)\\cap J_{\\max}$ has\n   $\\lVert z(t)\\rVert_{2}>M$, or $Q\\ne\\mathbb R^{3N}$ and the positions\n   approach the excluded configurations in the following sense: for every\n   $\\varepsilon,\\delta>0$ there are $t\\in(b-\\delta,b)\\cap J_{\\max}$ and\n   $y\\in\\mathbb R^{3N}\\setminus Q$ with\n   $\\lVert x_{\\max}(t)-y\\rVert_{2}<\\varepsilon$. If $Q=\\mathbb R^{3N}$, the\n   unbounded-state alternative necessarily holds.",
           "strategy": "A conditional deduction from the compact-continuation and escape theorems applied to the associated first-order system: a maximal motion with a finite endpoint leaves every compact subset of the open state domain, the autonomous case extends past a trapped endpoint, and the reading clause records the unbounded-state or excluded-configuration alternatives.",
