           "domain": "physics",
           "title": "Angular momentum balance about a fixed or moving reference point, the internal torque sum, and the centrality case",
-          "statement": "Adopt the force model and one inertial frame of\n[[def-cm03-centre-of-mass-and-momenta]], and suppose that the model invokes\nthe external-plus-pair presentation of clause (i) of [[post-cm02-interactions]]\nwith the antisymmetry clause (ii)1 (as in the momentum balance of this page).\nLet $x:J\\to Q$ be a motion on an interval $J$ with nonempty interior and let\n$o:J\\to\\mathbb R^{3}$ be a $C^{1}$ reference curve, with $J_{o}$,\n$T_{o}$, $T_{o}^{\\mathrm{ext}}$, $I$ and $P$ as in the definition. Then:\n\n1. **Internal torque identity.** For every $t\\in J$,\n   $$\\sum_{i}\\bigl(x_{i}(t)-o(t)\\bigr)\\times\\sum_{j\\ne i}F_{ij}\\bigl(t,x_{i}(t),x_{j}(t),w_{i}(t),w_{j}(t)\\bigr)=I(t),\\qquad\\text{so}\\qquad T_{o}(t)=T_{o}^{\\mathrm{ext}}(t)+I(t);$$\n2. **Balance.** $J_{o}$ is differentiable on $J$ and\n   $$J_{o}'(t)=T_{o}^{\\mathrm{ext}}(t)+I(t)-o'(t)\\times P(t)=T_{o}(t)-o'(t)\\times P(t)\\qquad(t\\in J);$$\n3. **Centrality.** Suppose that for every $i<j$ and every $t\\in J$ the\n   evaluated pair force is parallel to $x_{i}(t)-x_{j}(t)$, that is, there is\n   a real $\\lambda_{ij}(t)$ with\n   $$F_{ij}\\bigl(t,x_{i}(t),x_{j}(t),w_{i}(t),w_{j}(t)\\bigr)=\\lambda_{ij}(t)\\,\\bigl(x_{i}(t)-x_{j}(t)\\bigr).$$\n   Then $I\\equiv0$ on $J$, so\n   $$J_{o}'(t)=T_{o}^{\\mathrm{ext}}(t)-o'(t)\\times P(t)\\qquad(t\\in J);$$\n   if moreover $o$ is constant, then $J_{o}'=T_{o}^{\\mathrm{ext}}$;\n4. **Conservation.** If $o$ is constant and $T_{o}\\equiv0$ on $J$ —\n   equivalently $T_{o}^{\\mathrm{ext}}\\equiv-I$ there, by clause 1 — then\n   $J_{o}$ is constant on $J$. In particular, if the evaluated pair forces\n   are central (clause 3) and $T_{o}^{\\mathrm{ext}}\\equiv0$ on $J$ — the case\n   of a model with no external forces — then $T_{o}\\equiv0$ and $J_{o}$ is\n   constant on $J$.\n\nClause 3 allows cancellation: the vanishing of $I$ is a hypothesis on the\nevaluated pair forces and does not require centrality in general; the\ncompanion page exhibits antisymmetric non-central pair forces whose internal\ntorque sum vanishes identically. No empirical content is claimed.",
+          "statement": "Adopt the force model and one inertial frame of\n[[def-cm03-centre-of-mass-and-momenta]], and suppose that the model invokes\nthe external-plus-pair presentation of clause (i) of [[post-cm02-interactions]]\nwith the antisymmetry clause (ii)1 (as in the momentum balance of this page).\nLet $x:J\\to Q$ be a motion on an interval $J$ with nonempty interior and let\n$o:J\\to\\mathbb R^{3}$ be a $C^{1}$ reference curve, with $J_{o}$,\n$T_{o}$, $T_{o}^{\\mathrm{ext}}$, $I$ and $P$ as in the definition. Then:\n\n1. **Internal torque identity.** For every $t\\in J$,\n   $$\\sum_{i}\\bigl(x_{i}(t)-o(t)\\bigr)\\times\\sum_{j\\ne i}F_{ij}\\bigl(t,x_{i}(t),x_{j}(t),w_{i}(t),w_{j}(t)\\bigr)=I(t),\\qquad\\text{so}\\qquad T_{o}(t)=T_{o}^{\\mathrm{ext}}(t)+I(t);$$\n2. **Balance.** $J_{o}$ is differentiable on $J$ and\n   $$J_{o}'(t)=T_{o}^{\\mathrm{ext}}(t)+I(t)-o'(t)\\times P(t)=T_{o}(t)-o'(t)\\times P(t)\\qquad(t\\in J);$$\n3. **Centrality.** Suppose that for every $i<j$ and every $t\\in J$ the\n   evaluated pair force is parallel to $x_{i}(t)-x_{j}(t)$, that is, there is\n   a real $\\lambda_{ij}(t)$ with\n   $$F_{ij}\\bigl(t,x_{i}(t),x_{j}(t),w_{i}(t),w_{j}(t)\\bigr)=\\lambda_{ij}(t)\\,\\bigl(x_{i}(t)-x_{j}(t)\\bigr).$$\n   Then $I\\equiv0$ on $J$, so\n   $$J_{o}'(t)=T_{o}^{\\mathrm{ext}}(t)-o'(t)\\times P(t)\\qquad(t\\in J);$$\n   if moreover $o$ is constant, then $J_{o}'=T_{o}^{\\mathrm{ext}}$;\n4. **Conservation.** If $o$ is constant and $T_{o}\\equiv0$ on $J$ \u2014\n   equivalently $T_{o}^{\\mathrm{ext}}\\equiv-I$ there, by clause 1 \u2014 then\n   $J_{o}$ is constant on $J$. In particular, if the evaluated pair forces\n   are central (clause 3) and $T_{o}^{\\mathrm{ext}}\\equiv0$ on $J$ \u2014 the case\n   of a model with no external forces \u2014 then $T_{o}\\equiv0$ and $J_{o}$ is\n   constant on $J$.\n\nClause 3 allows cancellation: the vanishing of $I$ is a hypothesis on the\nevaluated pair forces and does not require centrality in general; the\ncompanion page exhibits antisymmetric non-central pair forces whose internal\ntorque sum vanishes identically. No empirical content is claimed.",
           "strategy": "A direct computation: reindex the internal pair torques using antisymmetry to obtain the internal-torque identity, differentiate the angular momentum by the product rule to get the fixed and moving-origin balance, and discharge the centrality and vanishing-total-torque hypotheses.",
           "deps": [
             "def-cm03-centre-of-mass-and-momenta",
@@ -23160,7 +23160,7 @@
           "kind": "definition",
           "domain": "physics",
           "title": "Potential presentations of a force model, potential and mechanical energy, closed versus exact force fields, local potentials and equipotential level sets",
-          "statement": "Adopt the force model and one inertial frame of\n[[def-cm03-centre-of-mass-and-momenta]], with configuration domain $Q$, time\nwindow $I_{0}$ and force map $F$. Dimensions are read by [[def-cm01-units]]\nand gradients are Euclidean ([[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]]); points of\n$Q$ are written $x=(x_{1},\\dots,x_{N})$ with $x_{i}\\in\\mathbb R^{3}$.\n\n**(a) Potential representation of the force map.** A model **presents its\nforce map through a potential** when it declares a function\n$$U:I_{0}\\times Q\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R$$\nof class $C^{2}$ ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]), of\ndimension $\\mu\\ell^{2}\\tau^{-2}$ (read in joules), with\n$$F_{i}(t,x,v)=-\\nabla_{x_{i}}U(t,x)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots,N)$$\nfor every $(t,x,v)$ of the state domain; here $\\nabla_{x_{i}}U$ is the\ngradient of $u\\mapsto U(t,x_{1},\\dots,x_{i-1},u,x_{i+1},\\dots,x_{N})$ at\n$u=x_{i}$. More generally, a model may declare **residual forces**\n$d_{i}:I_{0}\\times Q\\times(\\mathbb R^{3})^{N}\\to\\mathbb R^{3}$ of the standing\nregularity of clause (iii) of [[post-cm02-force-superposition]] and present\n$$F_{i}(t,x,v)=-\\nabla_{x_{i}}U(t,x)+d_{i}(t,x,v);$$\nthe pair $(U,d)$ is then the model's presentation and\n$F_{i}^{\\mathrm{p}}:=-\\nabla_{x_{i}}U$ is its **potential part**. The scalar\n$U$ is a **potential energy** for the model's potential part; the framework's\nearlier admission of potentials in [[post-cm02-interactions]] clause (iii) is\nthe special case $d\\equiv0$. A model that presents its whole force map this\nway with $d\\equiv0$ is a **global potential model** (the term \"conservative\nmodel\" is used only in this sense).\n\n**(b) Potential and mechanical energy along a curve family.** For a curve\nfamily $x:J\\to Q$ of the model ([[def-cm03-centre-of-mass-and-momenta]]\nclause (a)) define the **potential energy along the family** and the\n**mechanical energy** by\n$$U_{x}(t):=U\\bigl(t,x(t)\\bigr),\\qquad E(t):=K(t)+U_{x}(t)\\qquad(t\\in J),$$\nwith $K$ the kinetic energy of the definition. The potential part contributes\nto the force field but not directly to $K$; $E$ is defined only along the\npotential branch of the model, and along a motion it is the subject of the\nenergy balance of this page.\n\n**(c) Normalization of the potential.** Let $c:I_0\\to\\mathbb R$ be\ndifferentiable and put $\\widetilde U:=U+c(t)$, retaining $C^2$ regularity\nwhen a potential presentation of the standing class is required. The spatial\ngradient is unchanged, so this presents the same potential part with the same\nresidual forces. The full converse is componentwise: if $\\widetilde U$ and\n$U$ are $C^2$ and have the same spatial gradient, then on each connected\ncomponent $C$ of the open set $Q$ there is a $C^2$ function\n$c_C:I_0\\to\\mathbb R$ with\n$$\\widetilde U(t,x)-U(t,x)=c_C(t)\\qquad(x\\in C).$$\nHere a connected component is a maximal connected subset of $Q$. For fixed\n$t$, the difference is constant on every ball contained in $Q$: along any\nsegment in the ball its derivative is the vanishing spatial gradient paired\nwith the segment direction. The difference is consequently locally constant,\nand on a connected component it is constant, since a value's level set and\nits complement are both relatively open. Evaluating at any fixed point of\n$C$ gives the $C^2$ regularity of $c_C$. Conversely, independent such functions\non the components give a $C^2$ potential with unchanged spatial gradient;\ncomponents of an open Euclidean set are open, because each sufficiently small\nball about one of their points is connected and lies in that component.\nA single $c(t)$ describes every ambiguity precisely when the component\nfunctions agree, in particular when $Q$ is connected. No agreement follows\nfrom openness alone: on two disjoint balls the potentials $0$ and the function\nequal to $0$ on one ball and $1$ on the other have the same force. Every\ncontinuous motion on an interval remains in one component, so its energy\nshifts by that component's $c_C(t)$ and its energy rate by $c_C'(t)$.\nThe representative is part of the model's declaration, and every energy\nstatement names it.\n\n**(d) Closed and exact force fields.** A **time- and velocity-independent\nconfiguration-space force field** is a $C^{1}$ map\n$\\varphi=(\\varphi_{1},\\dots,\\varphi_{N}):Q\\to(\\mathbb R^{3})^{N}$, where\n$Q\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{3N}$ is the open configuration domain and each\n$\\varphi_i(x)$ is the force on label $i$ at configuration $x$; its components\nhave force dimension. It represents a force map of the form\n$F_i(t,x,v)=\\varphi_i(x)$ on the model's state domain. This configuration-space\nfield is not in general an external single-label force: to identify its\n$i$th component with an external map of [[post-cm02-interactions]] clause (i),\none must further require that it depend on $x_i$ alone, as in the declared\nsignature $F_i^{\\mathrm{ext}}(t,x_i,v_i)$. Such a field is **closed** when\n$\\partial_{k}\\varphi_{j}=\\partial_{j}\\varphi_{k}$ for all coordinate indices\n$j,k\\in\\{0,\\dots,3N-1\\}$ at every point of $Q$, using the flattened\ncoordinate indices of [[def-closed-and-exact-c1-vector-fields]], and **exact with potential\nenergy $U$** when $\\varphi=-\\nabla U$ for a $C^{2}$ function\n$U:Q\\to\\mathbb R$; equivalently, the field $-\\varphi$ is exact in the sense\nof [[def-closed-and-exact-c1-vector-fields]] with potential $U$. The following\nmathematical facts are used, with their proofs at the cited items:\n\n1. every exact field is closed, by equality of mixed partial derivatives\n   ([[thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials]], applied to the $C^2$ potential);\n2. on a star-shaped open domain every closed $C^{1}$ field is exact, by the\n   Poincaré lemma ([[thm-poincare-lemma-for-star-shaped-domains]]);\n3. beyond item 2 this item asserts **no** implication from closedness to\n   global exactness: on a general open domain a closed field need not have a\n   potential energy, and the criterion used by the framework is exactness as\n   defined above.\n\nConsequently the existence of a global potential energy on the model's\nconfiguration domain is a property of the field on that domain, and a model\nwhose potential part is a merely closed field on a non-star-shaped domain is\nnot thereby a global potential model. The distinction is sharp: the\ncompanion examples page carries a model on an annular domain whose force\nfield is smooth and closed, has nonzero circulation around a closed loop, and\ntherefore admits no global potential and no global conserved energy of the\nform $K+U$, while item 2 provides a local potential and a locally conserved\nenergy on every star-shaped subpart of that domain.\n\n**(e) Local potentials.** Let $V\\subseteq Q$ be open and star-shaped with\nrespect to one of its points ([[def-star-shaped-open-subset-of-rn]]) and let\n$\\varphi$ be closed on $V$. Then item 2 of (d) provides a $C^{2}$ potential\n$U_{V}:V\\to\\mathbb R$ with $\\varphi|_{V}=-\\nabla U_{V}$: a **local potential**\nof the field on $V$. On the restricted configuration domain $V$ the model\nwith force $-\\nabla U_{V}$ is a global potential model, so the mechanical\nenergy $K+U_{V}$ is available along curve families in $V$; this is how a\npotential is recovered from a merely closed field on a sufficiently small\nstar-shaped piece of the domain, with no claim of a global representative.\n\n**(f) Equipotential level sets.** For this clause assume additionally that\n$U:Q\\to\\mathbb R$ is smooth, meaning $C^k$ for every $k\\in\\mathbb N$\n([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]); the potential presentations\nof clause (a) still require only $C^2$. Let\n$c\\in\\mathbb R$ and suppose $U^{-1}(c)\\neq\\varnothing$ and $\\nabla\nU(x)\\neq0$ for every $x\\in U^{-1}(c)$; equivalently $c$ is a regular value of\n$U$ and every point of the level set is a regular point\n([[def-regular-and-critical-points-and-values]]). Then $U^{-1}(c)$ is an\nembedded submanifold of $Q$ of dimension $3N-1$\n([[thm-a-regular-level-set-is-an-embedded-submanifold]],\n[[def-embedded-submanifold-and-slice-chart]]). No such conclusion is made at a\nlevel where $\\nabla U$ vanishes somewhere on the level set, where the level\nset may have singular points.\n\n**(g) Status.** The potential, its normalization, the residual forces and the\ndeclaration of a potential presentation are model data; the closed/exact\nstatements of (d)-(f) are cited mathematics about the declared field. This\nitem asserts no conservation law, no force law of nature and no measured\nvalue.",
+          "statement": "Adopt the force model and one inertial frame of\n[[def-cm03-centre-of-mass-and-momenta]], with configuration domain $Q$, time\nwindow $I_{0}$ and force map $F$. Dimensions are read by [[def-cm01-units]]\nand gradients are Euclidean ([[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]]); points of\n$Q$ are written $x=(x_{1},\\dots,x_{N})$ with $x_{i}\\in\\mathbb R^{3}$.\n\n**(a) Potential representation of the force map.** A model **presents its\nforce map through a potential** when it declares a function\n$$U:I_{0}\\times Q\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R$$\nof class $C^{2}$ ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]), of\ndimension $\\mu\\ell^{2}\\tau^{-2}$ (read in joules), with\n$$F_{i}(t,x,v)=-\\nabla_{x_{i}}U(t,x)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots,N)$$\nfor every $(t,x,v)$ of the state domain; here $\\nabla_{x_{i}}U$ is the\ngradient of $u\\mapsto U(t,x_{1},\\dots,x_{i-1},u,x_{i+1},\\dots,x_{N})$ at\n$u=x_{i}$. More generally, a model may declare **residual forces**\n$d_{i}:I_{0}\\times Q\\times(\\mathbb R^{3})^{N}\\to\\mathbb R^{3}$ of the standing\nregularity of clause (iii) of [[post-cm02-force-superposition]] and present\n$$F_{i}(t,x,v)=-\\nabla_{x_{i}}U(t,x)+d_{i}(t,x,v);$$\nthe pair $(U,d)$ is then the model's presentation and\n$F_{i}^{\\mathrm{p}}:=-\\nabla_{x_{i}}U$ is its **potential part**. The scalar\n$U$ is a **potential energy** for the model's potential part; the framework's\nearlier admission of potentials in [[post-cm02-interactions]] clause (iii) is\nthe special case $d\\equiv0$. A model that presents its whole force map this\nway with $d\\equiv0$ is a **global potential model** (the term \"conservative\nmodel\" is used only in this sense).\n\n**(b) Potential and mechanical energy along a curve family.** For a curve\nfamily $x:J\\to Q$ of the model ([[def-cm03-centre-of-mass-and-momenta]]\nclause (a)) define the **potential energy along the family** and the\n**mechanical energy** by\n$$U_{x}(t):=U\\bigl(t,x(t)\\bigr),\\qquad E(t):=K(t)+U_{x}(t)\\qquad(t\\in J),$$\nwith $K$ the kinetic energy of the definition. The potential part contributes\nto the force field but not directly to $K$; $E$ is defined only along the\npotential branch of the model, and along a motion it is the subject of the\nenergy balance of this page.\n\n**(c) Normalization of the potential.** Let $c:I_0\\to\\mathbb R$ be\ndifferentiable and put $\\widetilde U:=U+c(t)$, retaining $C^2$ regularity\nwhen a potential presentation of the standing class is required. The spatial\ngradient is unchanged, so this presents the same potential part with the same\nresidual forces. The full converse is componentwise: if $\\widetilde U$ and\n$U$ are $C^2$ and have the same spatial gradient, then on each connected\ncomponent $C$ of the open set $Q$ there is a $C^2$ function\n$c_C:I_0\\to\\mathbb R$ with\n$$\\widetilde U(t,x)-U(t,x)=c_C(t)\\qquad(x\\in C).$$\nHere a connected component is a maximal connected subset of $Q$. For fixed\n$t$, the difference is constant on every ball contained in $Q$: along any\nsegment in the ball its derivative is the vanishing spatial gradient paired\nwith the segment direction. The difference is consequently locally constant,\nand on a connected component it is constant, since a value's level set and\nits complement are both relatively open. Evaluating at any fixed point of\n$C$ gives the $C^2$ regularity of $c_C$. Conversely, independent such functions\non the components give a $C^2$ potential with unchanged spatial gradient;\ncomponents of an open Euclidean set are open, because each sufficiently small\nball about one of their points is connected and lies in that component.\nA single $c(t)$ describes every ambiguity precisely when the component\nfunctions agree, in particular when $Q$ is connected. No agreement follows\nfrom openness alone: on two disjoint balls the potentials $0$ and the function\nequal to $0$ on one ball and $1$ on the other have the same force. Every\ncontinuous motion on an interval remains in one component, so its energy\nshifts by that component's $c_C(t)$ and its energy rate by $c_C'(t)$.\nThe representative is part of the model's declaration, and every energy\nstatement names it.\n\n**(d) Closed and exact force fields.** A **time- and velocity-independent\nconfiguration-space force field** is a $C^{1}$ map\n$\\varphi=(\\varphi_{1},\\dots,\\varphi_{N}):Q\\to(\\mathbb R^{3})^{N}$, where\n$Q\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{3N}$ is the open configuration domain and each\n$\\varphi_i(x)$ is the force on label $i$ at configuration $x$; its components\nhave force dimension. It represents a force map of the form\n$F_i(t,x,v)=\\varphi_i(x)$ on the model's state domain. This configuration-space\nfield is not in general an external single-label force: to identify its\n$i$th component with an external map of [[post-cm02-interactions]] clause (i),\none must further require that it depend on $x_i$ alone, as in the declared\nsignature $F_i^{\\mathrm{ext}}(t,x_i,v_i)$. Such a field is **closed** when\n$\\partial_{k}\\varphi_{j}=\\partial_{j}\\varphi_{k}$ for all coordinate indices\n$j,k\\in\\{0,\\dots,3N-1\\}$ at every point of $Q$, using the flattened\ncoordinate indices of [[def-closed-and-exact-c1-vector-fields]], and **exact with potential\nenergy $U$** when $\\varphi=-\\nabla U$ for a $C^{2}$ function\n$U:Q\\to\\mathbb R$; equivalently, the field $-\\varphi$ is exact in the sense\nof [[def-closed-and-exact-c1-vector-fields]] with potential $U$. The following\nmathematical facts are used, with their proofs at the cited items:\n\n1. every exact field is closed, by equality of mixed partial derivatives\n   ([[thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials]], applied to the $C^2$ potential);\n2. on a star-shaped open domain every closed $C^{1}$ field is exact, by the\n   Poincar\u00e9 lemma ([[thm-poincare-lemma-for-star-shaped-domains]]);\n3. beyond item 2 this item asserts **no** implication from closedness to\n   global exactness: on a general open domain a closed field need not have a\n   potential energy, and the criterion used by the framework is exactness as\n   defined above.\n\nConsequently the existence of a global potential energy on the model's\nconfiguration domain is a property of the field on that domain, and a model\nwhose potential part is a merely closed field on a non-star-shaped domain is\nnot thereby a global potential model. The distinction is sharp: the\ncompanion examples page carries a model on an annular domain whose force\nfield is smooth and closed, has nonzero circulation around a closed loop, and\ntherefore admits no global potential and no global conserved energy of the\nform $K+U$, while item 2 provides a local potential and a locally conserved\nenergy on every star-shaped subpart of that domain.\n\n**(e) Local potentials.** Let $V\\subseteq Q$ be open and star-shaped with\nrespect to one of its points ([[def-star-shaped-open-subset-of-rn]]) and let\n$\\varphi$ be closed on $V$. Then item 2 of (d) provides a $C^{2}$ potential\n$U_{V}:V\\to\\mathbb R$ with $\\varphi|_{V}=-\\nabla U_{V}$: a **local potential**\nof the field on $V$. On the restricted configuration domain $V$ the model\nwith force $-\\nabla U_{V}$ is a global potential model, so the mechanical\nenergy $K+U_{V}$ is available along curve families in $V$; this is how a\npotential is recovered from a merely closed field on a sufficiently small\nstar-shaped piece of the domain, with no claim of a global representative.\n\n**(f) Equipotential level sets.** For this clause assume additionally that\n$U:Q\\to\\mathbb R$ is smooth, meaning $C^k$ for every $k\\in\\mathbb N$\n([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]); the potential presentations\nof clause (a) still require only $C^2$. Let\n$c\\in\\mathbb R$ and suppose $U^{-1}(c)\\neq\\varnothing$ and $\\nabla\nU(x)\\neq0$ for every $x\\in U^{-1}(c)$; equivalently $c$ is a regular value of\n$U$ and every point of the level set is a regular point\n([[def-regular-and-critical-points-and-values]]). Then $U^{-1}(c)$ is an\nembedded submanifold of $Q$ of dimension $3N-1$\n([[thm-a-regular-level-set-is-an-embedded-submanifold]],\n[[def-embedded-submanifold-and-slice-chart]]). No such conclusion is made at a\nlevel where $\\nabla U$ vanishes somewhere on the level set, where the level\nset may have singular points.\n\n**(g) Status.** The potential, its normalization, the residual forces and the\ndeclaration of a potential presentation are model data; the closed/exact\nstatements of (d)-(f) are cited mathematics about the declared field. This\nitem asserts no conservation law, no force law of nature and no measured\nvalue.",
           "strategy": "A definition: it fixes potential presentations with residual forces, potential and mechanical energy along a curve family, the normalization under addition of a function of time, the closed/exact distinction for time- and velocity-independent fields with the cited Poincare result on star-shaped domains, local potentials on star-shaped subdomains and equipotential level sets.",
           "deps": [
             "def-cm03-centre-of-mass-and-momenta",
@@ -23431,7 +23431,7 @@
     },
     {
       "id": "cm03-momentum-work-and-energy-balances-examples",
-      "title": "Momentum, work and energy balances — Examples",
+      "title": "Momentum, work and energy balances \u2014 Examples",
       "kind": "B",
       "category": "non-relativistic-classical-mechanics",
       "library": "physics",
@@ -23742,7 +23742,7 @@
           "kind": "definition",
           "domain": "physics",
           "title": "The one-dimensional force models: data, motions, phase system, potential presentation, energy and equilibria",
-          "statement": "**(a) Data of a one-dimensional model.** A **one-dimensional model** is\ndeclared by the following data: a fixed positive **mass** $m>0$, of dimension\n$\\mu$ read in kilograms ([[def-cm01-units]], [[post-cm01-worldlines]] clause\n(v)); a **time window** $I_{0}\\subseteq\\mathbb R$, a nondegenerate open\ninterval read in seconds ([[def-interval]]); an open **configuration\ndomain** $Q\\subseteq\\mathbb R$, a nonempty open interval of the real line\nread in metres; and a **force law**\n$$F:I_{0}\\times Q\\times\\mathbb R\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R,\\qquad (t,x,v)\\longmapsto F(t,x,v),$$\nof dimension $\\mu\\ell\\tau^{-2}$, read in newtons, subject to the **standing\nregularity** of the framework: $F$ is continuous on $I_{0}\\times Q\\times\n\\mathbb R$ and locally Lipschitz in the state variable $(x,v)$ locally\nuniformly in time ([[def-locally-lipschitz-in-the-state-variable]]). The\nframework's three-dimensional force branch is\n[[post-cm02-force-superposition]]; the class declared here is a **restricted\nmodel class** adopted at the same primitive level, in which the physical\nposition of a single label along one coordinate is the whole state. A model\nwhose force law fails the standing regularity is not a one-dimensional model\nof this class. When a statement differentiates the force law or the potential,\nthe additional smoothness it needs is stated in that statement.\n\n**(b) Motions.** Let $J\\subseteq I_{0}$ be an interval with nonempty interior\n(endpoints read with one-sided derivatives, as in\n[[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]]). A **motion** of\nthe model on $J$ is a curve $x:J\\to Q$ of class $C^{2}$\n([[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]]) with\n$$m\\,x''(t)=F\\bigl(t,x(t),x'(t)\\bigr)\\qquad(t\\in J).$$\nIn the notation of the three-dimensional branch this is the scalar\nrestriction of its equation of motion clause (iv) to a single coordinate; the\nmotions of the present class are declared to be these curves. A **datum** at\ntime $t_{0}\\in I_{0}$ is a triple $(t_{0},x_{0},v_{0})$ with $x_{0}\\in Q$ and\n$v_{0}\\in\\mathbb R$; a motion $x$ **takes** the datum when $x(t_{0})=x_{0}$\nand $x'(t_{0})=v_{0}$.\n\n**(c) Phase system.** The **phase system** of the model is the first-order\nsystem on the open set $I_{0}\\times Q\\times\\mathbb R\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{3}$\nwith right-hand side\n$$G_{F}(t,x,v):=\\Bigl(v,\\ \\frac{F(t,x,v)}{m}\\Bigr),$$\nread as a system of [[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]];\nits state variable is $z=(x,v)$. For an interval $J\\subseteq I_0$ with\nnonempty interior, a **phase solution** on $J$ means a differentiable curve\n$z:J\\to Q\\times\\mathbb R$ satisfying $z'(t)=G_F(t,z(t))$ for every $t\\in J$,\nwith domain-relative derivatives at included endpoints. Equivalently, for\nany $t_0\\in J$ it is a solution in the cited IVP sense with datum\n$(t_0,z(t_0))$. For a $C^2$ curve $x:J\\to Q$, being a motion is equivalent\nto $t\\mapsto(x(t),x'(t))$ being a phase solution: the first component is\n$x'=v$ and the second is $x''=F(t,x,x')/m$. Conversely a phase solution\n$(x,v)$ has $x'=v$ and $v'=F(t,x,v)/m$; continuity of $F$ makes $v'$ continuous,\nso $x$ is $C^2$ and is a motion. This is the argument of clause (c) of\n[[def-cm02-newtonian-flow]] read in one\ncoordinate. Here $x$ is the **position** or **displacement** coordinate and\n$v$ the **velocity** coordinate; both are read in the fixed inertial frame in\nwhich the model is declared. The phase system inherits the standing\nregularity: $G_{F}$ is continuous and locally Lipschitz in the state variable\nlocally uniformly in time, so the Picard–Lindelöf hypotheses of\n[[thm-picard-lindelof-local-existence-and-uniqueness]] hold at every point of\nits domain.\n\n**(d) Potential presentation, residual forces and energy.** The model\n**presents its force law through a potential** when it declares a function\n$U:Q\\to\\mathbb R$ of class $C^{2}$ in the sense of clause (a) of\n[[def-cm03-potential-and-mechanical-energy]] of dimension\n$\\mu\\ell^{2}\\tau^{-2}$, read in joules, and a **residual force**\n$d:I_{0}\\times Q\\times\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ of the standing regularity of\n(a), with\n$$F(t,x,v)=-U'(x)+d(t,x,v)\\qquad\\bigl((t,x,v)\\in I_{0}\\times Q\\times\\mathbb R\\bigr);$$\nhere $U'$ is the ordinary derivative. This is the one-coordinate case of the\npotential presentation of [[def-cm03-potential-and-mechanical-energy]] clause\n(a) for a time- and velocity-independent potential, and the scalar\n$U$ is a **potential energy** of the model's potential part. The model is a\n**global potential model** (equivalently, in the framework's usage, a\n**conservative model**) when $d\\equiv0$; the term \"conservative\" is used only\nin this sense, as recorded in that definition. For a conservative model the\n**mechanical energy** is the function\n$$E:Q\\times\\mathbb R\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R,\\qquad E(x,v):=\\tfrac12 m v^{2}+U(x),$$\nof dimension $\\mu\\ell^{2}\\tau^{-2}$. For a model with declared residual\nforces, the **mechanical energy along a motion** $x:J\\to Q$ is\n$E(t):=\\tfrac12m\\,x'(t)^{2}+U(x(t))$; it is defined along curve families even\nthough the force law is not presented through $U$ alone.\n\n**(e) Equilibria.** A **time- and velocity-independent force law** on $Q$ is\none of the form $F(t,x,v)=f(x)$ for a function $f:Q\\to\\mathbb R$; the model\nis then **autonomous**. For such a law, a point $x_{*}\\in Q$ is an\n**equilibrium** (or **rest position**) when $f(x_{*})=0$, and the phase-plane\npoint $(x_{*},0)$ is the corresponding **rest point** of the phase system;\nthe constant curve $x\\equiv x_{*}$ is then a motion. When the force law is\npresented as $f=-U'$ with $U$ of class $C^{2}$, the equilibria are exactly\nthe critical points of $U$:\n$f(x_{*})=0\\iff U'(x_{*})=0$. A point $x_{0}\\in Q$ is a **strict local\nminimum** of $U$ when $U(x)>U(x_{0})$ for every $x\\in Q$ with $0<\\lvert\nx-x_{0}\\rvert<r_{0}$ for some $r_{0}>0$. The **Lyapunov stability** and\n**asymptotic stability** of a rest point of the autonomous phase system are\nthe notions of [[def-cm-equilibrium-and-lyapunov-stability]] applied to the\nfield $G_{F}$; they are not defined here anew.\n\n**(f) Oscillator-type data.** Let $m>0$, $c\\ge0$, $k\\in\\mathbb R$ and let\n$f:I_{0}\\to\\mathbb R$ be continuous. A model **is of oscillator type with\ndata $(m,c,k,f)$** when its force law has the form\n$$F(t,x,v)=f(t)-c\\,v-k\\,x\\qquad\\bigl((t,x,v)\\in I_{0}\\times Q\\times\\mathbb R\\bigr)$$\nwith $Q$ a nonempty open interval containing $0$ when the equation is read\nabout the origin, and the parameters are read with dimensions $[c]=\\mu\\tau^{-1}$\n(kilograms per second), $[k]=\\mu\\tau^{-2}$ (kilograms per second squared) and\n$[f]=\\mu\\ell\\tau^{-2}$ (newtons). For such a model the equation of motion is\nthe second-order equation $m\\,x''+c\\,x'+k\\,x=f(t)$, and the motions are\nexactly the restrictions of the solutions of the mathematical model of\n[[def-cm-oscillator-model-and-phase-system]] with the same data, by the\nidentification of that definition's phase system with the phase system (c).\nThe parameters $c$ and $k$ are model data: they enter the model's declaration\nand are not derived by this item.\n\n**(g) Status.** This item introduces vocabulary, objects, regularity and\ndimensions; it contains no existence, uniqueness, conservation, stability or\nboundedness assertion, no force law of nature and no measured value. In\nparticular, clause (a) **declares** the one-dimensional class rather than\nderiving it from the three-dimensional branch, and clauses (d)-(f) only fix\nwhich data a model of the class carries. Every later statement of this page\nnames the model data, the potential representative and the initial datum it\nuses.",
+          "statement": "**(a) Data of a one-dimensional model.** A **one-dimensional model** is\ndeclared by the following data: a fixed positive **mass** $m>0$, of dimension\n$\\mu$ read in kilograms ([[def-cm01-units]], [[post-cm01-worldlines]] clause\n(v)); a **time window** $I_{0}\\subseteq\\mathbb R$, a nondegenerate open\ninterval read in seconds ([[def-interval]]); an open **configuration\ndomain** $Q\\subseteq\\mathbb R$, a nonempty open interval of the real line\nread in metres; and a **force law**\n$$F:I_{0}\\times Q\\times\\mathbb R\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R,\\qquad (t,x,v)\\longmapsto F(t,x,v),$$\nof dimension $\\mu\\ell\\tau^{-2}$, read in newtons, subject to the **standing\nregularity** of the framework: $F$ is continuous on $I_{0}\\times Q\\times\n\\mathbb R$ and locally Lipschitz in the state variable $(x,v)$ locally\nuniformly in time ([[def-locally-lipschitz-in-the-state-variable]]). The\nframework's three-dimensional force branch is\n[[post-cm02-force-superposition]]; the class declared here is a **restricted\nmodel class** adopted at the same primitive level, in which the physical\nposition of a single label along one coordinate is the whole state. A model\nwhose force law fails the standing regularity is not a one-dimensional model\nof this class. When a statement differentiates the force law or the potential,\nthe additional smoothness it needs is stated in that statement.\n\n**(b) Motions.** Let $J\\subseteq I_{0}$ be an interval with nonempty interior\n(endpoints read with one-sided derivatives, as in\n[[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]]). A **motion** of\nthe model on $J$ is a curve $x:J\\to Q$ of class $C^{2}$\n([[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]]) with\n$$m\\,x''(t)=F\\bigl(t,x(t),x'(t)\\bigr)\\qquad(t\\in J).$$\nIn the notation of the three-dimensional branch this is the scalar\nrestriction of its equation of motion clause (iv) to a single coordinate; the\nmotions of the present class are declared to be these curves. A **datum** at\ntime $t_{0}\\in I_{0}$ is a triple $(t_{0},x_{0},v_{0})$ with $x_{0}\\in Q$ and\n$v_{0}\\in\\mathbb R$; a motion $x$ **takes** the datum when $x(t_{0})=x_{0}$\nand $x'(t_{0})=v_{0}$.\n\n**(c) Phase system.** The **phase system** of the model is the first-order\nsystem on the open set $I_{0}\\times Q\\times\\mathbb R\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{3}$\nwith right-hand side\n$$G_{F}(t,x,v):=\\Bigl(v,\\ \\frac{F(t,x,v)}{m}\\Bigr),$$\nread as a system of [[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]];\nits state variable is $z=(x,v)$. For an interval $J\\subseteq I_0$ with\nnonempty interior, a **phase solution** on $J$ means a differentiable curve\n$z:J\\to Q\\times\\mathbb R$ satisfying $z'(t)=G_F(t,z(t))$ for every $t\\in J$,\nwith domain-relative derivatives at included endpoints. Equivalently, for\nany $t_0\\in J$ it is a solution in the cited IVP sense with datum\n$(t_0,z(t_0))$. For a $C^2$ curve $x:J\\to Q$, being a motion is equivalent\nto $t\\mapsto(x(t),x'(t))$ being a phase solution: the first component is\n$x'=v$ and the second is $x''=F(t,x,x')/m$. Conversely a phase solution\n$(x,v)$ has $x'=v$ and $v'=F(t,x,v)/m$; continuity of $F$ makes $v'$ continuous,\nso $x$ is $C^2$ and is a motion. This is the argument of clause (c) of\n[[def-cm02-newtonian-flow]] read in one\ncoordinate. Here $x$ is the **position** or **displacement** coordinate and\n$v$ the **velocity** coordinate; both are read in the fixed inertial frame in\nwhich the model is declared. The phase system inherits the standing\nregularity: $G_{F}$ is continuous and locally Lipschitz in the state variable\nlocally uniformly in time, so the Picard\u2013Lindel\u00f6f hypotheses of\n[[thm-picard-lindelof-local-existence-and-uniqueness]] hold at every point of\nits domain.\n\n**(d) Potential presentation, residual forces and energy.** The model\n**presents its force law through a potential** when it declares a function\n$U:Q\\to\\mathbb R$ of class $C^{2}$ in the sense of clause (a) of\n[[def-cm03-potential-and-mechanical-energy]] of dimension\n$\\mu\\ell^{2}\\tau^{-2}$, read in joules, and a **residual force**\n$d:I_{0}\\times Q\\times\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ of the standing regularity of\n(a), with\n$$F(t,x,v)=-U'(x)+d(t,x,v)\\qquad\\bigl((t,x,v)\\in I_{0}\\times Q\\times\\mathbb R\\bigr);$$\nhere $U'$ is the ordinary derivative. This is the one-coordinate case of the\npotential presentation of [[def-cm03-potential-and-mechanical-energy]] clause\n(a) for a time- and velocity-independent potential, and the scalar\n$U$ is a **potential energy** of the model's potential part. The model is a\n**global potential model** (equivalently, in the framework's usage, a\n**conservative model**) when $d\\equiv0$; the term \"conservative\" is used only\nin this sense, as recorded in that definition. For a conservative model the\n**mechanical energy** is the function\n$$E:Q\\times\\mathbb R\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R,\\qquad E(x,v):=\\tfrac12 m v^{2}+U(x),$$\nof dimension $\\mu\\ell^{2}\\tau^{-2}$. For a model with declared residual\nforces, the **mechanical energy along a motion** $x:J\\to Q$ is\n$E(t):=\\tfrac12m\\,x'(t)^{2}+U(x(t))$; it is defined along curve families even\nthough the force law is not presented through $U$ alone.\n\n**(e) Equilibria.** A **time- and velocity-independent force law** on $Q$ is\none of the form $F(t,x,v)=f(x)$ for a function $f:Q\\to\\mathbb R$; the model\nis then **autonomous**. For such a law, a point $x_{*}\\in Q$ is an\n**equilibrium** (or **rest position**) when $f(x_{*})=0$, and the phase-plane\npoint $(x_{*},0)$ is the corresponding **rest point** of the phase system;\nthe constant curve $x\\equiv x_{*}$ is then a motion. When the force law is\npresented as $f=-U'$ with $U$ of class $C^{2}$, the equilibria are exactly\nthe critical points of $U$:\n$f(x_{*})=0\\iff U'(x_{*})=0$. A point $x_{0}\\in Q$ is a **strict local\nminimum** of $U$ when $U(x)>U(x_{0})$ for every $x\\in Q$ with $0<\\lvert\nx-x_{0}\\rvert<r_{0}$ for some $r_{0}>0$. The **Lyapunov stability** and\n**asymptotic stability** of a rest point of the autonomous phase system are\nthe notions of [[def-cm-equilibrium-and-lyapunov-stability]] applied to the\nfield $G_{F}$; they are not defined here anew.\n\n**(f) Oscillator-type data.** Let $m>0$, $c\\ge0$, $k\\in\\mathbb R$ and let\n$f:I_{0}\\to\\mathbb R$ be continuous. A model **is of oscillator type with\ndata $(m,c,k,f)$** when its force law has the form\n$$F(t,x,v)=f(t)-c\\,v-k\\,x\\qquad\\bigl((t,x,v)\\in I_{0}\\times Q\\times\\mathbb R\\bigr)$$\nwith $Q$ a nonempty open interval containing $0$ when the equation is read\nabout the origin, and the parameters are read with dimensions $[c]=\\mu\\tau^{-1}$\n(kilograms per second), $[k]=\\mu\\tau^{-2}$ (kilograms per second squared) and\n$[f]=\\mu\\ell\\tau^{-2}$ (newtons). For such a model the equation of motion is\nthe second-order equation $m\\,x''+c\\,x'+k\\,x=f(t)$, and the motions are\nexactly the restrictions of the solutions of the mathematical model of\n[[def-cm-oscillator-model-and-phase-system]] with the same data, by the\nidentification of that definition's phase system with the phase system (c).\nThe parameters $c$ and $k$ are model data: they enter the model's declaration\nand are not derived by this item.\n\n**(g) Status.** This item introduces vocabulary, objects, regularity and\ndimensions; it contains no existence, uniqueness, conservation, stability or\nboundedness assertion, no force law of nature and no measured value. In\nparticular, clause (a) **declares** the one-dimensional class rather than\nderiving it from the three-dimensional branch, and clauses (d)-(f) only fix\nwhich data a model of the class carries. Every later statement of this page\nnames the model data, the potential representative and the initial datum it\nuses.",
           "strategy": "A definition: it declares the one-dimensional model class (mass, time window, configuration interval, force law with the standing regularity), its motions and phase system, the potential presentation with residual forces and the mechanical energy, the equilibria and the strict-minimum vocabulary, and the oscillator-type data (m,c,k,f), with domains, regularity and dimensions; it adopts no force law and asserts no dynamical statement.",
           "deps": [
             "post-cm02-force-superposition",
@@ -23844,7 +23844,7 @@
           "kind": "postulate",
           "domain": "physics",
           "title": "The harmonic (linear restoring) model: a declared linear force law with parameter $k>0$",
-          "statement": "**(i) Setting.** Work in the one-dimensional model class of\n[[def-cm04-one-dimensional-models-and-equilibria]] with time window\n$I_{0}=\\mathbb R$, configuration domain $Q=\\mathbb R$, a fixed mass $m>0$\nand a **spring constant** $k>0$ of dimension $\\mu\\tau^{-2}$, read in newtons\nper metre ([[def-cm01-units]]). The **harmonic model** is the model whose\nforce law is the **linear restoring force**\n$$F(x,v):=-k\\,x .$$\nIts equation of motion is $m\\,x''=-k\\,x$, equivalently $m\\,x''+k\\,x=0$; the\nmodel is autonomous and velocity-independent, and its potential is\n$$U(x):=\\tfrac12 k\\,x^{2},$$\nof dimension $\\mu\\ell^{2}\\tau^{-2}$ (joules), which presents the force law in\nthe sense of clause (d) of [[def-cm04-one-dimensional-models-and-equilibria]]:\n$U$ is of class $C^{2}$ in that clause's sense, and\n$-U'(x)=-k\\,x=F(x)$ ([[lem-derivative-of-a-power]],\n[[def-derivative]]). The harmonic model is therefore a **conservative**\nmodel of the class, with the potential representative $U$ declared above and\nno residual force.\n\n**(ii) Regularity and framework status.** Let\n$X:=x_{\\rm phys}/(1\\,\\mathrm m)$ and\n$V:=v_{\\rm phys}/(1\\,\\mathrm{m/s})$ be the dimensionless numerical SI state\ncoordinates (the real state coordinates in the displayed force-map signature),\nand let $K:=k_{\\rm phys}/(1\\,\\mathrm{N/m})$ be the numerical\nspring constant. In this representation the numerical force map\n$\\widehat F:=F_{\\rm phys}/(1\\,\\mathrm N)$ is\n$\\widehat F(X,V)=-KX$, so\n$\\lvert\\widehat F(X,V)-\\widehat F(X',V')\\rvert\\le\nK\\,\\lVert(X,V)-(X',V')\\rVert_{2}$. It is therefore locally Lipschitz in the\nstate variable with global numerical-coordinate constant $K$, uniformly in\ntime ([[def-locally-lipschitz-in-the-state-variable]]). The harmonic model is\nthus an admissible model of the class of clause (a) of\n[[def-cm04-one-dimensional-models-and-equilibria]], and the existence,\nuniqueness and conservation statements of this page apply to it. It is\nadopted as a restricted model class of the framework, at the same primitive\nlevel as the force maps of [[post-cm02-force-superposition]]: the force law\nis model input.\n\n**(iii) Physical reading and regime.** The model idealizes a **restoring\nforce linear in the displacement**: an ideal spring, or the quadratic\napproximation of a potential $U$ near a strict local minimum $x_{*}$ with $U''(x_*)>0$, where\nthe Taylor expansion of the effective potential begins with the quadratic\nterm $\\tfrac12 U''(x_{*})(x-x_{*})^{2}$ and the linearized equation carries\nthe **effective spring constant** $U''(x_{*})$ (the sources' linearization\nprocedure). The model is adopted for a stated regime — displacements so\nsmall that the neglected remainder of the potential is adequate for the\npurpose at hand — and it is **not** claimed to describe large-amplitude\noscillations, nonlinear springs, plasticity, or any given real system. The\ncoefficient $k$ is a model parameter, not a constant of the framework; when\nits value is taken from a calibration experiment, that value is an empirical\npremise carrying the apparatus conditions and the measurement uncertainty,\nand the framework asserts none of it here. The linear form is the simplest\nrestoring law satisfying the standing regularity; that is a modelling\nconvenience, not a derivation.\n\n**(iv) Status.** Adopted assumption of the framework when a model invokes\nit; it selects no law of nature on its own. Every consequence below is a\nconditional statement about the declared equation of motion.",
+          "statement": "**(i) Setting.** Work in the one-dimensional model class of\n[[def-cm04-one-dimensional-models-and-equilibria]] with time window\n$I_{0}=\\mathbb R$, configuration domain $Q=\\mathbb R$, a fixed mass $m>0$\nand a **spring constant** $k>0$ of dimension $\\mu\\tau^{-2}$, read in newtons\nper metre ([[def-cm01-units]]). The **harmonic model** is the model whose\nforce law is the **linear restoring force**\n$$F(x,v):=-k\\,x .$$\nIts equation of motion is $m\\,x''=-k\\,x$, equivalently $m\\,x''+k\\,x=0$; the\nmodel is autonomous and velocity-independent, and its potential is\n$$U(x):=\\tfrac12 k\\,x^{2},$$\nof dimension $\\mu\\ell^{2}\\tau^{-2}$ (joules), which presents the force law in\nthe sense of clause (d) of [[def-cm04-one-dimensional-models-and-equilibria]]:\n$U$ is of class $C^{2}$ in that clause's sense, and\n$-U'(x)=-k\\,x=F(x)$ ([[lem-derivative-of-a-power]],\n[[def-derivative]]). The harmonic model is therefore a **conservative**\nmodel of the class, with the potential representative $U$ declared above and\nno residual force.\n\n**(ii) Regularity and framework status.** Let\n$X:=x_{\\rm phys}/(1\\,\\mathrm m)$ and\n$V:=v_{\\rm phys}/(1\\,\\mathrm{m/s})$ be the dimensionless numerical SI state\ncoordinates (the real state coordinates in the displayed force-map signature),\nand let $K:=k_{\\rm phys}/(1\\,\\mathrm{N/m})$ be the numerical\nspring constant. In this representation the numerical force map\n$\\widehat F:=F_{\\rm phys}/(1\\,\\mathrm N)$ is\n$\\widehat F(X,V)=-KX$, so\n$\\lvert\\widehat F(X,V)-\\widehat F(X',V')\\rvert\\le\nK\\,\\lVert(X,V)-(X',V')\\rVert_{2}$. It is therefore locally Lipschitz in the\nstate variable with global numerical-coordinate constant $K$, uniformly in\ntime ([[def-locally-lipschitz-in-the-state-variable]]). The harmonic model is\nthus an admissible model of the class of clause (a) of\n[[def-cm04-one-dimensional-models-and-equilibria]], and the existence,\nuniqueness and conservation statements of this page apply to it. It is\nadopted as a restricted model class of the framework, at the same primitive\nlevel as the force maps of [[post-cm02-force-superposition]]: the force law\nis model input.\n\n**(iii) Physical reading and regime.** The model idealizes a **restoring\nforce linear in the displacement**: an ideal spring, or the quadratic\napproximation of a potential $U$ near a strict local minimum $x_{*}$ with $U''(x_*)>0$, where\nthe Taylor expansion of the effective potential begins with the quadratic\nterm $\\tfrac12 U''(x_{*})(x-x_{*})^{2}$ and the linearized equation carries\nthe **effective spring constant** $U''(x_{*})$ (the sources' linearization\nprocedure). The model is adopted for a stated regime \u2014 displacements so\nsmall that the neglected remainder of the potential is adequate for the\npurpose at hand \u2014 and it is **not** claimed to describe large-amplitude\noscillations, nonlinear springs, plasticity, or any given real system. The\ncoefficient $k$ is a model parameter, not a constant of the framework; when\nits value is taken from a calibration experiment, that value is an empirical\npremise carrying the apparatus conditions and the measurement uncertainty,\nand the framework asserts none of it here. The linear form is the simplest\nrestoring law satisfying the standing regularity; that is a modelling\nconvenience, not a derivation.\n\n**(iv) Status.** Adopted assumption of the framework when a model invokes\nit; it selects no law of nature on its own. Every consequence below is a\nconditional statement about the declared equation of motion.",
           "strategy": "Adoption of the harmonic model: the linear restoring force -kx with declared spring constant k>0 and potential kx^2/2, with its regularity check, its reading as the quadratic approximation at a strict minimum of a potential, its regime of validity, and the status of k as model data.",
