-          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $N\\ge1$, use the open-chain Hilbert space and Hamiltonian $H_N(h,J)$ of the ordered Majorana definition, and let $\\beta>0$ have units $\\mathrm{J}^{-1}$ while $h,J$ have units joules. Define the real skew matrix $A_N\\in M_{2N}(\\mathbb R)$ by $ (A_N)_{2j-1,2j}=2h$ for $1\\le j\\le N$, $(A_N)_{2j,2j+1}=2J$ for $1\\le j<N$, the opposite entries by skew-symmetry, and all other entries by zero. Let $\\epsilon_1,\\ldots,\\epsilon_N\\ge0$ be its real orthogonal block energies, including zero values. Then\n$$H_N(h,J)=\\frac{i}{4}\\gamma^{\\mathsf T}A_N\\gamma=\\sum_{r=1}^N\\epsilon_r\\left(b_r^*b_r-\\frac12I\\right)$$\nfor the transformed Majorana modes $b_r$ in the Proof, and its finite Gibbs trace is\n$$Z_N(\\beta;h,J):=\\operatorname{Tr}_{\\mathcal H_N}(e^{-\\beta H_N(h,J)})=\\prod_{r=1}^N2\\cosh\\!\\left(\\frac{\\beta\\epsilon_r}{2}\\right).$$\nEvery zero mode contributes a factor two. The chain is open throughout; no periodic Jordan–Wigner parity boundary term is included.",
+          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $N\\ge1$, use the open-chain Hilbert space and Hamiltonian $H_N(h,J)$ of the ordered Majorana definition, and let $\\beta>0$ have units $\\mathrm{J}^{-1}$ while $h,J$ have units joules. Define the real skew matrix $A_N\\in M_{2N}(\\mathbb R)$ by $ (A_N)_{2j-1,2j}=2h$ for $1\\le j\\le N$, $(A_N)_{2j,2j+1}=2J$ for $1\\le j<N$, the opposite entries by skew-symmetry, and all other entries by zero. Let $\\epsilon_1,\\ldots,\\epsilon_N\\ge0$ be its real orthogonal block energies, including zero values. Then\n$$H_N(h,J)=\\frac{i}{4}\\gamma^{\\mathsf T}A_N\\gamma=\\sum_{r=1}^N\\epsilon_r\\left(b_r^*b_r-\\frac12I\\right)$$\nfor the transformed Majorana modes $b_r$ in the Proof, and its finite Gibbs trace is\n$$Z_N(\\beta;h,J):=\\operatorname{Tr}_{\\mathcal H_N}(e^{-\\beta H_N(h,J)})=\\prod_{r=1}^N2\\cosh\\!\\left(\\frac{\\beta\\epsilon_r}{2}\\right).$$\nEvery zero mode contributes a factor two. The chain is open throughout; no periodic Jordan\u2013Wigner parity boundary term is included.",
