h the first variation of the scheme-fixed generalized entropy exists and vanishes for every family $F$ specified in the preceding candidate-surface definition: $F$ is jointly smooth, each $F_\\epsilon$ is an admissible embedding with the same anchor and memberwise relative-chain homology condition, and its initial velocity $V$ satisfies $g(V,w)=0$ for every interior tangent vector $w\\in TX$ and extends smoothly by zero at the anchor. Let $\\mathcal Q_A$ be the prescribed class of such candidates for the chosen boundary state. Use SI units: $k_B$ is in $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$, $c$ is in $\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$, and $\\hbar$ is in $\\mathrm{J\\,s}$. Write $G_{\\Lambda_n}>0$ for the model-supplied Newton coupling at regulator label $\\Lambda_n$, in $\\mathrm{m^d\\,kg^{-1}\\,s^{-2}}$ for this $(d+1)$-dimensional bulk. When the chosen Einstein\u2013Hilbert branch uses the area term, use the same label-dependent physical-metric area $\\operatorname{Area}_{\\Lambda_n}[X]=\\int_{X^\\circ}\\chi_{\\Lambda_n,X}\\,d\\operatorname{vol}_{g|_{TX^\\circ}}$ defined by the candidate-surface model, with its explicit Countable Choice integration premise and its fixed finite cutoff data. This term is $k_Bc^3\\operatorname{Area}_{\\Lambda_n}[X]/(4G_{\\Lambda_n}\\hbar)$, and all additional finite terms remain in the prescribed $S_{\\mathrm{other},n}$. The cutoff area is in $\\mathrm{m^{d-1}}$, so $[c^3\\operatorname{Area}_{\\Lambda_n}[X]]=\\mathrm{m^{d+2}\\,s^{-3}}=[G_{\\Lambda_n}\\hbar]$ and the factor multiplying $k_B$ is dimensionless. The metric, Borel cutoff, label dependence and subtraction convention are thus fixed before the combined renormalized limit is tested; an unregulated area at the conformal boundary is not asserted finite. For $d=3$ these are the usual four-dimensional SI units. Adopt\n\n$$S_{\\partial}^{\\rm model}(A):=\\min_{X\\in\\mathcal Q_A}S_{\\rm gen}^{\\rm ren}[X]$$\n\nas the model's semiclassical boundary-entropy prediction only when $\\mathcal Q_A$ is nonempty, the values are finite, and the minimum is attained. It does not identify this prediction with $S_{\\rm exact}(\\rho_{\\partial})$. A **path-integral saddle** here is a stationary configuration of the chosen gravitational action; **saddle dominance** means that its contribution controls the specified path-integral approximation over competing configurations. Candidate existence, finite convergence, and dominance of the selected saddle are separate premises. The minimum rule is an adopted physical premise, not a consequence of the finite code entropy identity or the stationary Noether first law. It does not assert that the model describes nature, a universal RT/QES identity, or a continuum factorization of quantum fields.",
